{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>A man's joy is in the answer of his mouth, and a word in its season, how good it is (Proverbs 15:23).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> of blessed memory informs us in this verse (Proverbs 15) that when a man speaks his words with wisdom, he has joy on account of it, because speech comes from the aspect of the intellect by virtue of the rational soul in man, through which he has superiority over the rest of the animals. Therefore speech is attributed to the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is written (Isaiah 57:19), ״בורא ניב שפתים״ — 'creating the fruit of the lips'; and the prophet praises in this the Holy One, blessed be He, just as He praised Him for the creation of the heavens, which are themselves intelligible entities, as He said (Isaiah 42:5), ״בורא השמים ונוטיהם״. And he said that when a man intends to speak upright and well-ordered things and arranges them with the wisdom of the intellect, he will rejoice over this, because he did not bring forth the words on his lips until he arranged them in his heart. And it is known that the arrangement of things in a person's thought is in the person's hand, but speech is not so, for speech comes from HaShem — as it is written (Proverbs 16:1), ״לאדם מערכי לב ומה׳ מענה לשון״ — 'the arrangements of the heart are man's, but the answer of the tongue is from HaShem.' And when he directed the answer of his tongue and brought forth his words on his lips properly, this is a clear thing that it is by virtue of divine assistance, and joy will reach him from this — this is what it says: 'a man's joy is in the answer of his mouth.' Even though all the words he speaks are straight, it is not fitting for him to speak except 'a word in its season,' for one who would sing songs in a house of mourning or matters of mourning in a house of joy — how very disorderly and unfitting that would be — it is a thing not in its season, for they said: 'at a time of joy, joy; at a time of mourning, mourning.' And of this matter is what they said: one asks the laws of Passover at Passover, and the laws of Shavuot at Shavuot, and the laws of the Festival at the Festival. And one who would expound the laws of Sukkah at Passover or the laws of Passover at Sukkot — this too is a confused and mixed-up matter and is not 'a word in its season.' And of this Solomon of blessed memory here reminded us: 'A man's joy is in the answer of his mouth, and a word in its season, how good it is' — he means that even though a man speaks upright and true intellectual matters, he must overcome his desire and rule his spirit, not to speak of those uprightnesses except 'a word in its season.' This is by way of the plain sense (peshat)." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'A man's joy' — this is the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said (Exodus 15:3), ״ה׳ איש מלחמה״. 'In the answer of His mouth' — as it is said (Genesis 1:3), ״ויאמר אלהים יהי אור ויהי אור״. 'And a word in its season, how good it is' — as it is said (ibid.), ״וירא אלהים את האור כי טוב״. End." "<b>And it is known</b> that in the six days of Creation the verse mentioned ״כי טוב״ for each and every one of the six days, and whatever was created on each and every day was ״דבר בעתו״ — a thing in its season, even though the entire world in its totality, the upper ones and the lower ones, were created in a single instant — all this is true. And of this our Sages of blessed memory brought a parable: to a farmer who sowed six seeds in his hand at one instant, yet each and every one sprouted on its day, even though he sowed them all at once. And this is what Solomon of blessed memory said (Ecclesiastes 3:11): ״את הכל עשה יפה בעתו״ — 'He has made everything beautiful in its time.' And here he said, 'and a word in its season, how good it is': every single thing created on its day was in its season, and all was good — from light, which was first among the upper created beings, to man, who was last among the lower created beings — all was made in its proper time. And the last was last not for deficiency but for greatness and excellence, for the things last in deed were first in thought, and therefore the most excellent things you will find last. For man, who is the ultimate purpose in the creation of the world — to know his Creator and serve Him — was last of all created beings and chosen above them all. And so the Sabbath day was last of all the days and holiest of them all. And so the World to Come is last after this world, for this world precedes it, and a person cannot acquire the life of the World to Come except through the excellence of his deeds in this world. And so Israel were last in creation and first in thought, as it is written (Jeremiah 2:3): ״קדש ישראל לה׳ ראשית תבואתה״ — 'Israel is holy to HaShem, the first of His produce.' It does not say קדוש or קדושים but קדש, and this is to elevate the surpassing excellence of Israel's stature, for they draw from the Kedushah, as it is written (Exodus 22:30): ״ואנשי קדש תהיון לי״ — 'and you shall be men of holiness to Me' — to HaShem, as a portion for HaShem, as it is written (Deuteronomy 32:9): ״כי חלק ה׳ עמו״ — 'for HaShem's portion is His people' — not the portion of the stars and constellations, which are the portion of the nations. ״ראשית תבואתה״ — the choicest of His produce, like (Amos 6:6): ״וראשית שמנים ימשחו״ — 'and they anoint themselves with the first of oils.' Produce (תבואה) is five species: wheat, barley, oat spelt, rye, and spelt — therefore תבואתה is with a ה. And the choicest of the produce is wheat; so too Israel were the choicest of the nations, for they received the five books of the Torah, which is the choicest of Torahs. And of this they said in the Midrash: a parable — to a king who consecrated his wife with five rings, as it is said (Hosea 2:21–22): ״וארשתיך לי בצדק ובמשפט ובחסד וברחמים, וארשתיך לי באמונה וידעת את ה׳״. And this is the language of ראשית, which includes two kinds of priority: priority of excellence and priority in time. Priority of excellence — from (Amos 6:6) ״וראשית שמנים״; priority in time — from the language of (Genesis 10:10) ״ותהי ראשית ממלכתו בבל״. From this you learn that they are primordial and first in thought and last in creation, for Esau and his chieftains preceded Jacob and all his seed, as it is written of Esau (Genesis 25:25): ״ויצא הראשון אדמוני״. And also the genealogies of Esau preceded the genealogies of Jacob, for Esau took a wife at forty years of age. And the ultimate purpose was not in the earlier genealogies of Esau but in the genealogies of Jacob, which came afterward — and they were the beginning of the thought. And this is what David of blessed memory said (Psalms 40:6): ״נפלאותיך ומחשבותיך אלינו״, as the philosophers have said: 'the beginning of thought is the end of deed.' And the parable for this: Reuven wishes to study Torah and has no house of study. He will first need to lay the foundation of the building, raise the structure upon the foundation, and spread the roof over the building — and only then study there. And so the study comes last in deed but was first in thought. And this is like what our Sages of blessed memory expounded (Deuteronomy 2:19): ״אל תצורם ואל תתגר בם״ — 'do not besiege them and do not provoke them' — why did the verse warn thus concerning Ammon and Moab? Because of two fine doves that I am destined to bring forth from them: Ruth the Moabite and Naamah the Ammonite. From all this you learn that the last is the most excellent, and what was created last arose first in thought. What is the matter like? Like one who lost a pearl in the sand: he brings a sieve and sifts all the sand until he finds the pearl. And so we find in plants: if you take a seed and sow it, it takes root in the ground and sends roots here and there until a great tree grows from it, from which emerges the fruit, which is hidden and concealed within it. And it is known that the fruit, which comes last, was the main intention in the sowing of the seed and the growing of the tree — and it was the beginning of the thought. And just as we find in the work of Creation that the beasts and wild animals and trees and plants preceded the formation of man — yet he was first and last, as it is written (Psalms 139:5): ״אחור וקדם צרתני״, as if to say 'first and last You have formed me' — first in excellence and last in creation. And as soon as He created him He placed in his hand a positive commandment and a negative commandment, so too we find that the Torah arranged three sections: one concerning beasts and wild animals, which belong to the element of earth — ״זאת החיה אשר תאכלו״; a second concerning fish, which belong to the element of water — ״את זה תאכלו מכל אשר במים״; a third concerning birds, which belong to the element of spirit — ״ואת אלה תשקצו מן העוף״. And last He mentioned the formation of man, and immediately imposed upon him a commandment — and it is circumcision — to make known that the essential purpose of man's birth in this world is nothing but the commandment, as it is written (Job 5:7): ״כי אדם לעמל יולד״ — 'for man is born to toil' — that is, to the toil of Torah and commandments. Therefore the Torah placed his formation in the midst of the commandments and surrounded him with them, before and after. Before him — these are the dietary commandments; and so our Sages of blessed memory expounded: what is the meaning of ״אחור וקדם צרתני״? After He created all the beasts and birds and creeping things and swarming things, He created him. Thus this child — before he emerges from his mother's womb, the Holy One, blessed be He, says: 'I command him — this you shall eat and this you shall not eat; and this is impure to you; this you shall eat and this you shall not eat'; and once he accepts upon himself all the commandments in the Torah, afterward he is born. After him — this is the commandment of circumcision. And this is what is written." "<b>If a woman conceives.</b> It would have been fitting for the verse to say 'if a woman gives birth to a male,' but the word תזריע comes to include one who gives birth to a fetus formed in human shape that afterward becomes unviable, so that the mother is impure by birth, as Rashi of blessed memory explained." "<b>And from</b> this word did the rabbis of blessed memory derive: 'a woman who emits seed first gives birth to a male; a man who emits seed first — she gives birth to a female.' And the intent of the term מזרעת ('emits seed') concerns the seed of the female, which is the blood of menstruation, for the woman has no seed; rather, the blood that is in her womb is her seed, and it is called אודם ('redness'), while the seed of the male is called לובן ('whiteness'), and the אודם and the לובן, each of them, is called 'seed.' And so the rabbis of blessed memory said at the end of the chapter HaMapelet: 'three partners there are in a person — the Holy One, blessed be He, his father, and his mother; his father emits לובן, from which come brain and sinews and bones and nails and the לובן that is in the eye; his mother emits אודם, from which come skin and flesh and blood and hair and the black of the eye; and the Holy One, blessed be He, gives into him spirit and soul and the countenance of the face and sight of the eye and hearing of the ear and speech of the lips and walking of the feet and knowledge and understanding and reason.' And the word תזריע is derived therefrom." "<b>And by way of the plain sense (peshat)</b> its meaning is: she shall deliver the seed, for it is a deposit with her from the male, like the seed of the field which is a deposit in the body of the field and comes forth in its season. And from this the verse says (Exodus 21:22): ״כאשר ישית עליו בעל האשה״ — 'as the husband of the woman shall impose upon him' — not 'as the woman shall impose upon him,' for the children are his, and he is the one fit to set a valuation upon them. And I have already written there." "<b>But</b> the philosophers of nature say that the entire body of the fetus belongs to the woman, and the man has in it only the force called in their language 'hylé,' which gives form to the matter; for the seed, when it mingles with the blood of the woman, gives it form and organization, and separates from it the superfluities that would not succeed in the fetus, and coagulates it — in the manner that the curdling of milk coagulates. And they have proofs for their view. The first: from the egg of the hen, which raises a chick when it comes from the male; but when it comes from those that roll in the dust, it raises no chick at all, because the male is absent from it — and he is the one who gives the form. And so is the view of the rabbis of blessed memory in the first chapter of tractate Yom Tov: יהבו ליה ביעי דספנא מארעא אזל אותבינהו ולא אפריחו — 'they gave him a wooden egg from the ground; he went and sat upon them, and they did not hatch.' The second: from certain birds that reproduce by embrace alone, and no emission of seed is seen in them, and this is proof that the seed of the male is not a part of the fetus. The third: from the female fish that lays eggs on her own, and afterward, when the male casts his seed upon them, whatever the seed falls upon becomes a fish, and all the eggs upon which it does not fall do not become fish at all. And this is found not in animals alone, but also in plants: for if you take a male date-palm and graft it with the female palm, then it will succeed in producing fruit. And the reason is that the male gives warmth to the female, by which the ripening of the dates is completed. This is the view of the wise man, the head of the philosophers, together with some of his proofs." "<b>And the reason</b> for the matter — 'a woman who emits seed first gives birth to a male; a man who emits seed first, she gives birth to a female' — is that when the woman emits seed first, the seed of the male, which comes last, comes and overpowers it, and therefore she gives birth to a male, for דעילאה גבר — 'the upper one prevails.' If the man emits seed first, the drop of the female comes last and overpowers it, and therefore she gives birth to a female. And the principle is that the first drop, whether from the male or from the female, is like the field that is planted, and the last drop is like the seed that is sown in the field. And since when the woman emits seed first, that drop is like a field, and the drop of the male that comes last is the seed, therefore she bears a male like him — and this is the sense of תזריע. And if the male emits seed first, that drop is like a field, and the drop of the female that comes last is the seed, and she bears a female like her." "<b>And by way of the science of nature:</b> 'a woman who emits seed first gives birth to a male' — for when the woman desires her husband and is heated with passion for him, all her desire and thought are upon him, and she pictures his form in her heart, and out of that passion she hastens and emits seed first; and through the force of that pictured thought in her heart she bears a male corresponding to her heart and eyes. And this is the matter of the rods for our father Jacob, with which he made drawings, and the flock, when they were heated, conceived in the pattern of those forms that they saw at the time of mating — and in this males and females were alike. And the same is so for the human species, all the more so, for if in the bovine species there is power in the pictured thought upon one, how much more so in the rational species; and I have already mentioned this regarding the rods. And whoever is able to master his impulse and holds himself back so that she emits first — he receives his reward, for she bears a male for this reason. And this is what the rabbis of blessed memory derived from the verse (Psalms 127:3): ״הנה נחלת ה' בנים שכר פרי הבטן״ — 'Behold, the heritage of HaShem is sons; the reward of the fruit of the womb' — these are those who hold themselves back upon the stomach, so that their wives emit first and bear males; and this is 'the reward of the fruit of the womb.'" "<b>And some</b> further explained: when the woman emits seed first she gives birth to a male, because there is a hidden power in the woman's seed, namely the male power, and therefore she gives birth to a male. Just as there is a hidden power in the male, namely the female blood — and the proof is from the first man: Chavah was built from him and was hidden within his power, so too the male power is hidden within the female's seed. And just as the female was emanated from man, so too it is fitting that the male be emanated from her: if the woman emits seed first, she gives birth to a male, which is the power hidden within her; and if the male emits seed first, she gives birth to a female — the power hidden within him. Thus it is clear that in both of them there is power to establish the complete structure." "<b>And you already know</b> that the Name was not mentioned in its fullness in all of the work of Creation until it reached the formation of man. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted on (Genesis 2:7) ״וייצר ה׳ אלהים את האדם״ — the full Name, concerning a full world. And how is it possible for them to say 'a full world' when the woman had not yet been created? Rather, her power was hidden within him, and it is as though she was already created, and thus the world was full. And the proof that each one of them is a full world is that just as the full Name is invoked for the formation of man, as it says ״וייצר ה׳ אלהים״, so too the full Name is invoked for the woman, as it says (ibid.) ״ויבן ה׳ אלהים את הצלע״. If so, each one is fit to establish this complete structure, for this one is included in that one and that one in this one. And we find a full verse (Genesis 46:15): ״אלה בני לאה אשר ילדה ליעקב ואת דינה בתו״ — the males he attributed to Leah, but the female he attributed to Yaakov. And this is clear: that the male power is hidden in the female just as the female power is in the male. Not that the male begets a male and the female a female, for concerning this they said in the Yerushalmi: לית ספר דמספר לגרמיה — no book accounts for itself." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> ״אשה כי תזריע וילדה זכר״ — if the woman preceded, she gives birth to a male, as it says ״אשה כי תזריע וילדה זכר״; if the man preceded, she gives birth to a female, as it says ״ואם נקבה תלד״. The Holy One, blessed be He, performs an act of wonders for this infant. A man placed in prison for one day — his soul convulses to get out; yet this infant is confined in his mother's womb for nine months, and the Holy One, blessed be He, guards him. Rabbi Meir said: the Holy One, blessed be He, performed an act of wonders for this infant — how so? Before the woman gives birth she draws blood, and when she gives birth it withdraws to the breasts and becomes milk, and the infant nurses from it." "<b>By way of reason (sekhel):</b> Three worlds pass over a person. The first is when he is in his mother's womb, and it is called the world of formation (yetzirah), and it is the beginning of his existence, and in it a person recognizes the wonders of the Creator, may He be exalted, and the greatness of His deeds. This is what David said concerning it (Psalms 139:15): ״אשר עשיתי בסתר רקמתי בתחתיות ארץ״, and earlier he had already mentioned (ibid. 14): ״אודך על כי נוראות נפליתי נפלאים מעשיך״ — for he is speaking of the wonders of wisdom and of the hidden things that are revealed and made clear through the structure of the formation. And from this the Men of the Great Assembly established for us at the beginning of our blessings each day: ״אשר יצר את האדם בחכמה״, and its seal: ״ומפליא לעשות״. And of this Kohelet said (Ecclesiastes 11:5): ״כאשר אינך יודע מה דרך הרוח כעצמים בבטן המלאה ככה לא תדע את מעשה האלהים אשר יעשה את הכל״ — he says that all the deed of Elohim is hidden and wondrous, and he brings proof from the perceptible, namely the fetus in its mother's womb, which is called עצמים, and it is a hidden matter that follows after the miraculous: how it is sustained within it, and what is the cause that pushes it to leave there. And he likened this to דרך הרוח to explain that it is a hidden thing like the hiding of the way of the spirit; and just as the way of the spirit and the עצמים בבטן המלאה are hidden, concealed things, so too all the deed of Elohim is hidden and concealed — the power of apprehension falls short of it. And concerning this world he said (Job 10:10): ״זכר נא כי כחמר עשיתני וגו׳, הלא כחלב תתיכני וכגבינה תקפיאני, עור ובשר תלבישני ובעצמות וגידים תשוככני״. And this is the world in which a person stands for a fixed time — nine months and sometimes later, and occasionally as long as twelve months. There he has no intellect or knowledge and no necessity at all; by his own will he would remain there forever, for there his sustenance is available to him from the mother's nourishment. And when he departs from there he is forced and compelled — and this is what the Sages of blessed memory said: על כרחך אתה נוצר — against your will you are formed; and therefore he cries and suffers at birth, to indicate that this world is a world of suffering and turmoil. From that world of formation he is transported to the second world, which is this world, and in it he has a great advantage over the world he came from. For in this world, just as his body grows, so too his intellect grows and his wisdom increases, until he becomes wise in Torah and in commandments, and through them he attains knowledge of HaShem, may He be exalted. And even in this world, in which he has the advantage of all this glory and these excellences, he stands there for a fixed time, as it says (Psalms 90:10): ״ימי שנותינו בהם שבעים שנה ואם בגבורות שמונים שנה״. And when he departs from this world he is forced and compelled — and this is what the Sages of blessed memory said: על כרחך אתה חי ועל כרחך אתה מת — against your will you live and against your will you die; for by his will he would stand there forever, for eternity. From this world he is transported to the third world, which is the World to Come, which has no fixed time, and there is no end or limit to the delight of the one who attains it. In it the reward of his righteousness that he did in this world is paid to him in full." "<b>If so,</b> it is clear that a person goes from good to good and from excellence to excellence through these three worlds: the first is the world of formation, which is darkness and no light; the second, this world, has in it both darkness and light, for it is conducted according to the two luminaries; the third is the World to Come, which is entirely good, entirely long, entirely light with no darkness in it. And these three worlds Kohelet, peace be upon him, alluded to in a single verse, namely (Ecclesiastes 7:1): ״טוב שם משמן טוב ויום המות מיום הולדו״, and its interpretation according to this approach is: טוב — the treasure that is good for the soul above, namely the treasures of merit for the World to Come; משמן טוב — that is, the treasure for the body, namely treasures of wealth and good oil and other acquisitions of this world, as it is written (Proverbs 21:20): ״אוצר נחמד ושמן בנוה חכם״. And טוב יום המות — which is this world — מיום הולדו — that is, more than the world of formation. He comes to explain that the World to Come has an advantage over this world, just as this world — which is יום המות — has an advantage over the world of formation; for from within the perceptible and the tangible to the eye we can testify concerning that which is not tangible." "<b>And she shall be impure seven days.</b> It is a decree of Scripture that the impurity is seven and the purity likewise seven, for the pure days in the seventh grade are from below upward; also the rejoicing is seven, and the mourning likewise seven, and all of them follow one way." "<b>As in the days of the menstruation of her infirmity she shall be impure.</b> דותה (her infirmity) is an expression of מדוה (illness), for menstruation is an illness in a woman's nature, and so the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: her head and limbs feel heavy upon her. And although it is a cleansing for the woman from what is superfluous, nevertheless it is an illness by nature of her condition. And the term נדה (menstruant) denotes distance and removal, as in the expression מנודה (excommunicated), which means removed: 'we keep away from you so as not to sit within your four cubits.' And she is called נדה because people shun and distance her; also the women keep away from her, and she sits alone. And so we find from earliest times that the nations would keep them at a great distance, and even the dust upon which the menstruant treads was impure in their eyes; also her speech was impure to them, as we find with Rachel, who said to her father Laban: 'Let it not be displeasing in my lord's eyes that I cannot rise before you, for the way of women is upon me' — and he did not reply to her at all, which indicates that they would sit alone in a special tent, for the practice was not to speak with them, and they were also not permitted to approach any person at all because their odor and breath are harmful; for were it not so, it would have been proper for Rachel to rise before her father and approach him and kiss his hands. And it is known that even her glance is harmful and breeds injury. And the philosophers of nature have already explained among the wonders of natural phenomena that if a menstruant looks at the onset of her menstruation at a polished sword or into a mirror, drops of blood will appear upon it, and this is a sign and wonder that even her glance is harmful — needless to say if a man lies with her."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {7 "'היולי' transliterates the Aristotelian term for the generative/formative force (Greek hylē or the hyletic principle); rendered as in the original. The Aramaic citation from BeitYahor, page 65a/b (chapter 1) is given verbatim.", 11 "The Aramaic quotation from Yerushalmi Niddah is rendered as written, untranslated, following the faithful-literal rule for cited rabbinic text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:26:34.774819196Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}